# Keep the build context small and keep secrets out of it.
#
# Everything here is either regenerated inside the image, irrelevant to it, or must
# never be copied in. A large context is slow to send to the daemon and invalidates
# layer caches for no reason.

# Dependencies are installed from the lockfile inside the image, and a host
# node_modules would bring the wrong platform's binaries with it — Prisma's engine
# in particular is compiled per OS.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output, all regenerated.
.next
**/.next
dist
**/dist
out
**/out
next-env.d.ts

# Secrets. An .env baked into a layer stays readable in the image history even if a
# later layer deletes it, and an image gets pushed to a registry.
#
# The `**/` forms are the load-bearing ones and were added after catching this for
# real: dockerignore patterns are matched against the path from the context root, so
# a bare `.env` matches only `/.env` and sails straight past `apps/api/.env`. That
# file — with the database URL and the signing secret in it — was found inside a
# built image. Do not simplify these back to one line.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Version control and tooling state.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github
.claude
.impeccable
.agents

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Documentation and local notes: not part of the running app.
*.md
!README.md

# Commercial material, kept out of images as well as out of the repo.
sales-package.html
sales-package-spanish.html

# Docker's own files do not belong inside the image.
Dockerfile
**/Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.*.yml
